.TH PAM_FACIAL_AUTH 8 "2026-11-17" "facial-auth 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pam_facial_auth \- PAM module for face authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pam_facial_auth.so
[\fBdebug\fR] [\fBquiet\fR] [\fBallow_locked\fR] [\fBmode=\fR\fIMODE\fR]
[\fBsocket=\fR\fIPATH\fR] [\fBmarker_dir=\fR\fIDIR\fR] [\fBmax_rescans=\fR\fIN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Provides the \fBauth\fR service.  The module asks
.B facial-authd
(over a root-owned Unix socket whose peer credentials it verifies) whether the
face in front of the camera belongs to the user being authenticated.  It only
returns \fBPAM_SUCCESS\fR when the daemon answers "OK"; in every other case it
returns \fBPAM_IGNORE\fR so that the rest of the stack (\fBpam_unix\fR...)
handles the password.  A failed face scan therefore never counts as a failed
login.
.PP
The module is enabled by \fBpam-auth-update\fR(8).  It should be placed before
\fBpam_unix\fR with the control
.B [success=end conv_err=die default=ignore].
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B debug
Log details through syslog.
.TP
.B quiet
Do not print "Looking for your face..." messages.
.TP
.B allow_locked
Also try the face for accounts whose password is locked (\fBpasswd -l\fR).  By
default such accounts never use their face.
.TP
.BI mode= MODE
Override the interaction mode chosen by the daemon: \fBsequential\fR,
\fBparallel\fR or \fBparallel-tty\fR.
.TP
.BI socket= PATH
Daemon socket (default \fI/run/facial-auth/pam.sock\fR).
.TP
.BI marker_dir= DIR
Directory with one file per enrolled user; users without a file never reach
the daemon (use \fBmarker_dir=-\fR to disable).
.TP
.BI max_rescans= N
How many times an empty password restarts the face scan (default 3).
.SH INTERACTION MODES
.TP
.B sequential
The scan comes first; the password prompt follows if it fails.  Safe
everywhere; the default at the GNOME login screen.
.TP
.B parallel
The password prompt is displayed while the camera scans.  If the face wins, the
pending prompt is abandoned.  For graphical helpers (polkit).
.TP
.B parallel-tty
Like \fBparallel\fR for terminals (sudo, su, login): a pending prompt is
cancelled and the terminal state restored when the face wins.  An empty
password restarts the scan; Ctrl-C aborts at once.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.TP
.B PAM_SUCCESS
The daemon recognised the face.
.TP
.B PAM_CONV_ERR
The user abandoned the password prompt (use \fBconv_err=die\fR).
.TP
.B PAM_IGNORE
Anything else.
.SH SECURITY
Remote sessions (\fBPAM_RHOST\fR, ssh environment), closed laptop lids, locked
accounts and unlisted services never use the camera.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR facial-auth (1),
.BR pam.conf (5),
.BR pam-auth-update (8)
